(* agent note (|2026-10-16 FED3 PROOFS WRAP-UP (board card
        todo/g4-fed3-proofs; answers PP run-primitive M12's NEXT line
        "allowlist, proofs?").  The structural proof is the journaling
        law itself: every fed-apply answer - applied, shadowed, refused,
        denied - is a journaled op row in the tree_ops chain (the apply
        answer's op_append, the deny paths' op_append), so a
        verification or attribution failure can never be absorbed
        silently; the chain IS the receipt, and F2's
        rejects-are-journaled clause holds at the same boundary.  Since
        M12 landed, the floor tightened on every side a bridge write
        could ride: g1 (2026-10-16) makes agent tree ops deny-all with
        explicit covering grants at the direct API too (the
        operator-side-door stance is retired); a2 (2026-10-16) makes
        fed apply authorize and verify the window BEFORE it writes, so
        a denied apply plants nothing; g2 (2026-10-16) adds per-runner
        revocable Bearer tokens (the glochid issue-token law) - the
        runner authenticates as its own identity at full bearer parity,
        which is exactly the attribution F3 demands.  WHAT PROOFS
        REMAIN: F3 is still "not yet exercised" by its own
        pre-registration - the one open obligation is an exercised
        attribution probe, positive (a bridge write through a runner
        token attributes to that identity: run rows carry caller,
        journal rows carry the actor, the ops chain carries the
        fed-apply row) and negative (any attempt to write outside
        ns/<peer>/ or through a revoked token is refused AND journaled,
        never absorbed).  Until that probe is pinned as a verify gate,
        F3 stays open by the MEASURE-don't-massage rule; the allowlist
        half of M12's question is answered on the tuna side by g1+g2
        (deny-all floor + per-runner allowlists are drivable end to
        end).|) *)
 (section federation
  (doc "Three phases, each independently shippable, each with pre-registered failure. FED1 implemented 2026-09-18; FED2 implemented 2026-10-01; FED3 re-opened 2026-10-13 (PP acted_by shipped; tuna half rehearsed live; PP half = bearer parity + the prims library).")
  (status in-progress)
  (subsection phases
   (status in-progress)
   (details
    (doc (|
      FED1 VALUE EXCHANGE (read-only, IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-18). GET
         /api/fed/value/:hash returns {hash, kind, payload} for either
         kind (tree ternary, bytes base64; a program hash resolves as
         its own ternary); the peer rehashes on receipt and trusts
         nothing but the hash (scripts/fed-pull.sh). Per-peer bearer
         identities via TUNA_FED_PEERS; hash-gated reads (byte-values
         law 2) mean no new authz model; fetches journal as op
         "fed-value" attributed to the calling identity.
      FED2 OPS-CHAIN SYNC (IMPLEMENTED 2026-10-01). Pull: GET
         /api/fed/ops?prefix=&after_seq= returns a contiguous op window;
         each row carries its true global predecessor hash so
         prefix-filtered (non-contiguous) windows verify per row
         (verify_window_rows; the d7e0ae7 lesson generalized - a window
         needs an anchor, which a carried prev_hash supplies for every
         row). Push: POST /api/fed/ops/apply re-verifies the window,
         rehashes inline values, checks per-peer authz (peer <name> ->
         ns/<name>/...; admin any), and folds it into the destination
         atomically. Append-only, no merge: a re-apply shadows under a
         fresh version; divergence is two namespaces, never silently
         reconciled.
      FED3 PRICKLYPEAR BRIDGE. Align attribution first (PP acted_by,
         still unshipped), then bearer parity (PP delegated Bearer ==
         tuna identity token), then a PP prims library calling
         /api/tree/* and /api/fed/* so PP rows and tuna paths cite each
         other by id/hash without either store encoding the other's
         internals. PP is a client of the tree surface before it is a
         peer of the chain. |))))
  (subsection dictionary-visibility
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A deliberate narrowing of the FED2 ops window: the LO2 instance
      local.borg L5/L6 record, which must land with or before L5 and
      never after.  A dictionary is private to its identity by
      default, with a per-identity public option, so moving
      definitions into tree_ops never widens who can read them.  The
      window is already prefix-filtered; it also becomes
      ownership-filtered.  No route, request field or query parameter
      is added, and visibility is never a caller-supplied input: a
      caller cannot ask to see a private row.  No released writer
      creates dict/ paths today, so on a normal instance the filter
      changes nothing until L5; fed apply can always plant
      ns/<peer>/dict/..., which the filter then hides.  Shipping the
      filter before L5 is safe, and shipping L5 first is not.

      FILTER.  A window row names a dictionary when its path is
      dict/<identity>/... or ns/<peer>/dict/<identity>/... .  The
      identity is the single path segment between dict/ and the next
      slash; the trailing slash is required, so dict/A and dict/AB/...
      are not A's rows, and an empty identity matches no caller (fail
      closed).  A row that names a dictionary is served only when the
      caller is that identity, the caller is admin
      (identities.is_admin), or that identity's dictionary visibility
      is public.  Every other row is served exactly as FED2 serves it
      today: the filter removes rows and never adds one.  A vis/ row
      (below) names no dictionary and is served to any caller as an
      ordinary row: the flag is not a dictionary.

      SETTING.  Visibility is per identity and private when no setting
      exists.  The REPL command is dict-visibility public|private,
      which sets the caller's own dictionary; an admin may also set
      another identity's flag with dict-visibility <identity>
      public|private, still a REPL command value that adds no route or
      request field and that a non-admin cannot use for another
      identity.  The row's actor is the caller and the path names the
      target, which a non-admin may only set to itself; on the server
      the caller is the authenticated identity, and on the offline CLI
      it is the local identity L2 fixes at directory creation, so a
      local call is self-only.  The command appends one op to the
      local chain: op kind dict-visibility, path vis/<identity>/public
      or vis/<identity>/private, no value hash (it carries no effect,
      so fed apply lands it as a cas proof row like any other
      non-effect).  The effective setting is the last such row by seq;
      a past setting is the fold up to that seq, so a change is
      auditable and rewindable from the log alone.  A put or cas at
      vis/... by any other writer is ignored: only a dict-visibility
      row sets the flag, which is what makes "changed only through
      this command" true.  It is a LOCAL setting: a vis/ row that
      arrives through fed apply lands under ns/<peer>/vis/... and
      changes nothing here, so no peer can publish another identity's
      dictionary.  The dict REPL command is unchanged: it still lists
      only the caller's own rows, whatever the visibility is.

      PAGING AND VERIFICATION.  The ownership filter runs after the
      seq window is read and after verify_window_rows, so last_seq is
      the last row of the UNFILTERED window: a page still advances
      past hidden rows, and a caller cannot use paging to stall.  more
      is computed from the unfiltered window length, full or not,
      before ownership filtering, so it can be true with no visible
      rows and can be true on the last page.  Chain verification is
      over the unfiltered window, so a tampered hidden row still fails
      the page (verified:false, 500); that failure names the bad row's
      seq as any FED2 failure does.  Hidden rows are omitted, never
      trusted.  The fed-ops journal row records the caller and the
      requested prefix as before.

      WHAT PRIVACY DOES NOT COVER.  Values remain hash-gated reads
      (byte-values law 2): a private dictionary hides which names an
      identity binds to which hashes, and its history; it does not
      hide a tree whose hash has leaked.  The chain still shows that
      hidden rows exist: seq gaps and head reveal their count and
      timing, and the op_hash of a hidden row is carried as the next
      served row's prev_hash, so someone who can guess that hidden
      row's every field can confirm the guess.  The def-usage census
      (/api/defs/usage) also stays public to authenticated callers: it
      lists a name and the identities defining it, with no hash and no
      history.  Narrowing the census is a separate decision, not part
      of this filter.  Accepted, as local.borg L6 states; the section
      claims no more.

      ACCEPTANCE: with identity A private, a pull by B at any prefix
      contains no dict/A/... row, still answers verified:true (the
      rows it does carry verify per row), and its last_seq advances
      past the omitted rows; an admin pull carries them; after A runs
      dict-visibility public, B's next pull carries them and still
      verifies; with A private and peer P having applied rows at
      ns/P/dict/A/..., B's pull of ns/ or ns/P/ omits them, P's own
      pull of ns/P/ omits them, and A's pull carries them; a pull that
      selects no dictionary rows carries the rows and last_seq FED2
      would answer, with verified:true; a page whose window is all
      hidden answers more:true with an empty op list; a put or cas at
      vis/A/public by an ordinary tree write leaves A's visibility
      unchanged; a push of vis/A/... by peer P lands at ns/P/vis/A/...
      and changes nothing. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. Two peers diverge on the same path and no check notices ->
         failure; divergence must surface as distinct namespaces or a
         verified rejection.  FED2 holds: ns/<peer>/ scoping keeps
         namespaces distinct, and a re-apply of an existing path
         SHADOWS under a fresh version (reported in the apply answer's
         "shadowed" list), never a silent merge.
      2. An applied op that fails rehash or chain-linkage verification
         -> failure; rejects are journaled, never absorbed.  FED2 holds:
         every pulled row carries its true prev_hash and apply
         re-verifies each row (tampered = 400, journaled fed-apply); a
         value-citing effect whose value is not held locally is also
         refused (400), value-pull-before-ops is the caller's law.
        3. A bridge write attributed to any identity other than the true
           actor (or its delegated identity) -> failure.  (FED3 EXERCISED
           2026-10-16: scripts/verify-14-f3-attribution.sh pins positive
           attribution - a peer's own ops row and run row carry its
           identity, and a spoofed-actor fed apply re-attributes at the
           boundary, ops.actor and tree_paths.owner are the true actor,
           server-derived, the client never sets actor fields - and the
           negatives (out-of-namespace apply refused AND journaled,
           never absorbed; token revocation via verify-1 [1.7]).  FED3
           holds.
        4. A row naming a dictionary is served to a caller who is
           neither that identity nor admin, and no check notices ->
           failure.  The ownership filter (dictionary-visibility)
           holds only if its acceptance is exercised: a second
           identity's pull, a peer's pull and an admin's pull must pin
           the omission and the inclusion, for both the dict/ and
           ns/<peer>/dict/ forms. |))))))
